<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antibes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Antipolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antibes is a town on the French Riviera between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannes">Cannes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>. It began as Antipolis, a Greek trading settlement founded by Massalia, facing the Ligurian peoples of the hills behind it. When the Deciates and Oxybii besieged it in 154 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> intervened on Massalia's behalf. It later became a Roman town and, in modern times, a fortified French port on the border with Savoyard Nice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antibes.jpg" alt="Antibes" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilbert Bochenek · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antibesbordmer.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Antibes lies on the French Riviera in the Alpes-Maritimes, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannes">Cannes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>. Its cape, the Cap d'Antibes, is one of the area's two major landforms, with Cap Ferrat to the northeast. The resort of Juan-les-Pins is within the commune, and the Sophia Antipolis technology park lies northwest of it. There are 48 beaches along 25 km of coastline around Antibes and Juan-les-Pins. Port Vauban, with more than 2,000 moorings, is the largest yachting harbour in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its old port was the heart of the ancient Greek city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Traces of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> occupation have been found in the area of the castle and cathedral. An indigenous community had ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> peoples, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, as the underwater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> and wrecks off Antibes show, though most trade was with the Greek world through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaeans</a> of Marseille. Antibes was founded as a Greek colony by Phocaeans from Massalia, probably in the 4th century BCE, and named Antipolis, Opposite-City, from its position across the Var estuary from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>.</p><p>The Bishopric of Antibes was established c. 450 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-i">Pope Leo I</a>. In the 10th century raiders from Muslim Spain menaced the coast until Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-provence">William I of Provence</a> captured their stronghold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraxinetum">Fraxinetum</a> in 975. He gave the liberated lands to his knights, and one of them, Rodoald, became Lord of Antibes.</p><p>Saracen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> burned the cathedral in 1124 and kept preying on the town, and in 1244 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> moved to Grasse, where they stayed for five centuries. In 1383 Marie de Blois confiscated the lordship from the bishops of Grasse and awarded it two years later to the Genoese brothers Marc and Luc Grimaldi, who built the Château Grimaldi. Provence passed to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1481, which made Antibes a border town facing the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, then part of the Savoyard state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-doria">Andrea Doria</a> sacked it in 1536, and Henry II ordered the building of Fort Carré in 1550, later reinforced by Vauban.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>From December 1746 an Austro-Savoyard army under Maximilian Ulysses Browne besieged Antibes. It lifted the siege on 1 February 1747, but its guns had levelled 350 houses and destroyed the cathedral again, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> later rebuilt. On 1 March 1815 Napoléon Bonaparte landed at Golfe-Juan, 5 km to the southwest, on his return from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>. He hoped for a warm welcome at Antibes, but the townspeople closed their gates to him. Antibes was transferred from Var to Alpes-Maritimes in 1860. In 1926 the municipality bought the Château Grimaldi, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a> lived for six months in 1946; it now houses the Musée Picasso.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antibes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Deciates were a Ligurian people living in the hills behind <b>Antipolis</b> (Antibes), a colony of Massalia.</blockquote><a href="/state/deciates">Deciates</a></li><li><blockquote>Refugees arrived after Phocaea fell to Persia in 545 BCE, and Massalia planted daughter towns such as Nikaia (Nice), <b>Antipolis</b> (Antibes) and Emporion in Spain.</blockquote><a href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a></li><li><blockquote>Known chiefly from Polybius, the Oxybii lived on the coast west of <b>Antibes</b>, near the port of Aegitna, probably around modern Mandelieu or Cannes.</blockquote><a href="/state/oxybii">Oxybii</a></li></ul></section>
